<section>
  <h2>The Full Catalog</h2>

  <p class="lede">All 51 items, grouped by the pass that found them. Classifications:
  <span class="kind">DEFECT</span> a live problem introduced into code or data ·
  <span class="kind">CLAIM</span> something asserted that was not true when asserted ·
  <span class="kind">PROCESS</span> a working-method failure ·
  <span class="kind">COST</span> compute or time wasted.</p>

  <h3>Pass 1 — 16 items. Errors surfaced during the session, nearly all by the operator</h3>

  <table class="catalog">
    <thead><tr><th>#</th><th>What went wrong</th></tr></thead>
    <tr><td>1</td><td><span class="kind">PROCESS</span> Omitted the handoff's process section ("a green suite is not evidence") from the opening summary, though it was in context and in memory.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>2</td><td><span class="kind">CLAIM</span> Concluded two bug records were never logged. Both were the top two entries. The search tool skips files it deems binary and exits as though nothing matched.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>3</td><td><span class="kind">DEFECT</span> Re-introduced a raw control byte into the bug log while fixing raw control bytes, because the editing tool converts an escape into the byte it denotes.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>4</td><td><span class="kind">CLAIM</span> Reported a byte count of 1332 that was actually the line count, because the shell strips the byte from the search pattern.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>5</td><td><span class="kind">CLAIM</span> A byte scan reported hundreds of false positives that were dash characters, an artifact of the locale chosen for the scan.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>6</td><td><span class="kind">PROCESS</span> Claimed three-engine coverage from two engines on the host and one in a container, against a written project rule requiring all engines in one container.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>7</td><td><span class="kind">PROCESS</span> Truncated a background run's output before reading it, destroying the detail needed to verify which tests ran. A full re-run was required.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>8</td><td><span class="kind">CLAIM</span> Asserted a container image "was never corrupted" from a matching digest. A digest proves identity, not the integrity of extracted bytes.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>9</td><td><span class="kind">PROCESS</span> Deleted the old image before diagnosing, making an old-versus-new comparison permanently impossible.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>10</td><td><span class="kind">CLAIM</span> Read four clean runs as a repair, supported by a probability invented from three observations assuming independence between runs that visibly cluster.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>11</td><td><span class="kind">CLAIM</span> Quoted a failure rate of 55% when the data already held gave 64%.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>12</td><td><span class="kind">PROCESS</span> Treated a crash as a yes-or-no signal across four rounds of experiments. The full crash output contained the answer from its first occurrence.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>13</td><td><span class="kind">PROCESS</span> Wrote two test-fixture bugs and briefly suspected the production code instead.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>14</td><td><span class="kind">DEFECT</span> Wrote tests against an invented function signature that does not exist.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>15</td><td><span class="kind">PROCESS</span> Ran the end-to-end suite against a stale build predating the fix and nearly concluded the fix did not work.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>16</td><td><span class="kind">PROCESS</span> Nearly reported a document as sixteen pages; the output directory held stale images from a different document.</td></tr>
  </table>

